## Preliminaries: check your setup

Evaluate this cell after setup finishes. A **satisfiable** result showing `ready`
confirms that the bundled clingo is running inside the notebook. Each solve
closes its own native control; this check is safe to reevaluate.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("../support/course.exs", __DIR__))
alias ASPCourse, as: Course
alias Clingo.Control

Clingo.solve!("ready. #show ready/0.") |> Course.display()
```

## Change inputs and ground new parts

### What you will learn

Reuse a grounded control without confusing source addition with grounding.

### Toggle an external input

An external atom is an input whose truth can be assigned between solves. It begins false, becomes true, and becomes false again. The three returned snapshots keep their original shown atoms.

```elixir
snapshots =
  Clingo.with_control(fn control ->
    Clingo.Control.add!(control, "#external switched. lit :- switched. #show lit/0.")
    Clingo.Control.ground!(control)
    off = Clingo.Control.solve!(control)
    Clingo.Control.assign_external!(control, "switched", true)
    on = Clingo.Control.solve!(control)
    Clingo.Control.assign_external!(control, "switched", false)
    off_again = Clingo.Control.solve!(control)
    [off, on, off_again]
  end)

[[], ["lit"], []] = Enum.map(snapshots, fn result -> hd(result.models).shown end)
Course.display(Enum.at(snapshots, 1))
```

### Ground a parameterized part deliberately

add! stores source for a named part; ground! instantiates that part for supplied symbol arguments. Ground step(1), solve, then ground step(2) and solve again. Use fresh atom instances rather than redefining a grounded head.

```elixir
snapshots =
  Clingo.with_control(fn control ->
    Clingo.Control.add!(control, "step", ["t"], "seen(t). #show seen/1.")

    Enum.map(1..2, fn step ->
      Clingo.Control.ground!(control, [{"step", [Clingo.Symbol.number(step)]}])
      Clingo.Control.solve!(control)
    end)
  end)

[["seen(1)"], ["seen(1)", "seen(2)"]] =
  Enum.map(snapshots, fn result -> hd(result.models).shown end)

Course.display(List.last(snapshots))
```

### Try it yourself

Extend the part loop from 1..2 to 1..3. Predict all three snapshots.

<details>
<summary>Hint and expected result</summary>

Their shown sets contain seen(1); then seen(1), seen(2); then seen(1), seen(2), seen(3). Earlier copied snapshots do not gain later atoms.

</details>

## Reuse a control between solves

An external atom can change truth assignment between solves without rebuilding the whole program. The first workflow compares its false, true and free assignments. Each named result belongs to a distinct solve of the same scoped control.

Assumptions apply to a particular search; they are not permanent facts. Later cells contrast both signs of an assumption with an unconstrained solve. Adding a constraint changes the grounded program, whereas releasing an external makes its external assignment unavailable for future changes. All related steps live inside a bracketed control so native state closes safely.

```elixir
Clingo.with_control([models: :all], fn control ->
  Control.add!(control, "base", [], "#external a. b :- not a.")
  Control.ground!(control)
  a = Control.literal(control, "a")
  initially_false = Control.solve!(control)
  Control.assign_external!(control, a, true)
  assigned_true = Control.solve!(control)
  Control.assign_external!(control, a, :free)
  %{initially_false: initially_false, assigned_true: assigned_true, open: Control.solve!(control)}
end)
```

> **Note.** Adding a. after grounding would redefine a head in clingo 5.8.2. Add :- not a. to constrain the existing choice program instead. Both steps share one scoped session.

```elixir
Clingo.with_control([models: :all], fn control ->
  Control.add!(control, "base", [], "{a}. b :- not a.")
  Control.ground!(control)
  a = Control.literal(control, "a")
  false_assumption = Control.solve!(control, assumptions: [-a])
  true_assumption = Control.solve!(control, assumptions: [a])
  no_assumption = Control.solve!(control)
  Control.add!(control, "new", [], ":- not a.")
  Control.ground!(control, [{"new", []}])

  %{
    false: false_assumption,
    true: true_assumption,
    open: no_assumption,
    added_program: Control.solve!(control)
  }
end)
```

```elixir
Clingo.with_control([models: :all], fn control ->
  Control.add!(control, "base", [], "{a}. b :- not a.")
  Control.ground!(control)
  Control.add!(control, "assumption1", [], ":- a, flag1. #external flag1. [true]")
  Control.ground!(control, [{"assumption1", []}])
  first = Control.solve!(control)
  Control.release_external!(control, Control.literal(control, "flag1"))
  Control.add!(control, "assumption2", [], ":- not a, flag2. #external flag2. [true]")
  Control.ground!(control, [{"assumption2", []}])
  second = Control.solve!(control)
  Control.release_external!(control, Control.literal(control, "flag2"))
  %{first: first, second: second, released: Control.solve!(control)}
end)
```
